<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anzac Day</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">25 April</span> · <a href="/today-in-history/april-25">On this day: 25 April</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anzac Day, 25 April, is the national day of remembrance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, marking the anniversary of the landing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-and-new-zealand-army-corps">Australian and New Zealand Army Corps</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> in 1915. First observed in 1916, it now commemorates all who served and died in military operations, with dawn services, marches and ceremonies in both countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anzac-day.jpg" alt="Anzac Day" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gnangarra · CC BY 2.5 au · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawn_service_gnangarra_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1915 Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> soldiers formed part of an Allied expedition that set out to capture the Gallipoli Peninsula in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and open the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australian-and-new-zealand-army-corps">Australian and New Zealand Army Corps</a> landed on 25 April and met fierce resistance from the Ottoman Army commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>, later known as Atatürk.</p><p>The campaign became a stalemate and dragged on for eight months. At the end of 1915 the Allied forces were evacuated after heavy casualties on both sides. Allied deaths totalled over 56,000, including 8,709 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and 2,721 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. News of the landing made a profound impact at home. The first Anzac Day marches were held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and Australia in 1916; the historian Martin Crotty has argued that they served political purposes, chiefly recruiting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Anzac Day is a national day of remembrance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, observed on 25 April, and a public holiday in both and in Tonga. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it came to commemorate those lost in that war and in later conflicts, and its meaning has since broadened to all who served and died in military operations. It was first marked at the Australian War Memorial in 1942 and has been observed there every year since.</p><p>The dawn service is timed to 4:28 am, when the landing began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a>. An Australian battalion held one on the Western Front on 25 April 1916, and services sprang up spontaneously in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in later years. The service at the Sydney Cenotaph in 1928 can claim to be the start of a continuous tradition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Interest has varied. By the 1950s many New Zealanders were antagonistic or indifferent, partly because of the legal ban on commerce and on sport and entertainment; a new Anzac Day Act in 1966 allowed sport and entertainment in the afternoon.</p><p>Services are also held abroad, among them at Villers-Bretonneux in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and Hellfire Pass in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The name ANZAC Cove was officially recognised by the Turkish government in 1985, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> the word Anzac is protected by the Protection of Word Anzac Act 1920. The day has met criticism too: from the labour movement at its inception, and in Alan Seymour's 1960 play The One Day of the Year, which questioned old values and drunken celebration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anzac Day” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The landing of the Australian and New Zealand Army Corps at Gallipoli on 25 April 1915, commemorated as <b>Anzac Day</b>, became a founding national legend, and the Australian Corps under Sir John Monash fought with distinction on the Western Front in 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
